From "ease of use" onwards:

"make quickcheck"
	will do a few quick checks.
	This just covers basic xmodem and zmodem functionality.

"make vcheck"
	will do many more tests.
        this test suite is moderately complete. There are still some options
        without tests, but all crucial ones are covered.

"make qa"
        will run something similar to
             configure && make clean && make && make vcheck
        multiple times:
        - gcc and/and clang, if available, multiplied with
        - ASan, MSan und UBSan.
        and a final coverage run.
        
        If this runs through, a final
             configure && make clean && make
        is run.

The checks will $TMP/testdir.lrzsz, and will not remove the directory
afterwards. In it you will found, among things i didn't clean up:
- passfail.log (shows all tests run with PASSED/FAILED info)
- failures.log (shows all failed tests)
- perf.log (performance data)

Note that the tests are run over a pipe and a named pipe. The performance
values are in no way comparable to a real serial interface (or even a 
network connection).
Having said that: in real life the number will change, but what is faster
in the benchmark will most likely be faster over a real serial line.


If you want to do compatibility checks:
- if this condition is true, compatibility checks against zmtx-zmrx are
  run:
        test -x ~/bin/zmtx -a -x ~/bin/zmrx

- if this condition is true, compatibility checks against lrzsz-0.12.21rc
  are run:
        test -x ~/bin/lrzsz-0.12.21rc/lrz -a -x ~/bin/lrzsz-0.12.21rc/lsz

- if this condition is true, compatibility checks against crzsz are run:
        test -x ~/bin/crzsz/crz -a -x ~/bin/crzsz/csz
  crzsz was the latest omen technology x/y/zmodem for unix.


